  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 100 AM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nWednesday night through Sunday:\n\nSynopsis: High pressure will linger over the western Great Lakes \nthrough Thursday, maintaining a cooler and dry weather pattern. The \nhigh will shift east Friday into Saturday, allowing for southerly \nwinds and steadily climbing surface temperatures through the \nweekend. Increasing humidity will eventually accompany the rising \ntemperatures, though its arrival will lag behind that of the warmer \nair temperatures. More humid conditions will thus hold off until \nSaturday night into Sunday. With warmer temperatures & humidity \nattempting to work back into the area, shower and thunderstorm \nchances return to the forecast Saturday night into Sunday.\n\nSaturday through Sunday: The next period of potential weather \nimpacts arrives following a quiet end to the work week. Impacts \ncould come from a combination of increasing humidity & heat indices \non Sunday, in addition to chances for showers and thunderstorms \nSaturday night into Sunday. Finer details---namely if heat indices \nwill approach headline thresholds and/or if any storms could be \nstrong/severe---will be refined as trends clarify through the end of \nthis week, though initial indications are that clouds & limited air \nmass residence time will keep temps & heat indices below headline \nlevels. Any stronger storm potential will depend on timing, the \ndetails of which remain murky this far out. Will provide more \nupdates as this portion of the period draws closer.  \n\nQuigley",
